#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083139 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083337 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef670158-f161-51f8-b146-0c07f8a1fa5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type and Strength of a Given Correlation"}] :question/id #uuid "87541606-5e53-5e3c-aefa-b3d6a6a12961" :question/math-academy-id "e-9696" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa9f4ce130811186b476064a3d0b828daf9f5ded3d9011eda832f3bc470f8334.png)\n\nThe scatterplot above shows the correspondence between the mean daily temperature outside a particular coffee shop and the number of customers ordering hot drinks. Which of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b9/b970e539af5b695644486ad4eb7c21558c334ffd33c1df1a2343fac493d84389.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *weak* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *weak negative* linear correlation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083140 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b973aca4-f9fc-5b5c-ab75-2329204993ce" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Correlation Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "8aaa2e17-cd20-5d59-80c5-6ec3c7842672" :question/math-academy-id "e-9697" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4e3c58da0dc7f326497d9a7c1327d6d40f98684089694f9de88f21a64f4b4824.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the correlation coefficient for the data in the scatter plot above?\n\nI. $\\rho =-0.8$\n\nII. $\\rho= 0.3$\n\nIII. $\\rho = 0.9$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/06/06dcc617bbbc07fea756f35975146a238bd4cb9c4ce437f1b9300a7c05ded7b6.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *positive*. Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho = -0.8\n$$\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $1.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho = 0.3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient from the given options is $\\rho= 0.9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083141 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbc6b77-cd04-5509-b344-66feddfed1bb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Scatter Plot Given a Correlation Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "5e64e6c3-80f5-5b23-90fa-8512ad377948" :question/math-academy-id "e-9698" :question/problem "It is known that there is a linear correlation between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho\\approx -0.85,$ sketch a possible scatter plot that corresponds to this situation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\approx -0.85,$ there is a *strong negative* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *negative* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *negative* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should lie *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation could correspond to the following scatter plot:\n\n![](images/ad/ad8ff5e49649b5e788b83eb28fa26afae61072d5e63026631a9cd1891be14a0c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083337 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef670158-f161-51f8-b146-0c07f8a1fa5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type and Strength of a Given Correlation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0660e208-70e5-5b04-ad6a-42881363a6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "strong negative linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545212} {:answer/id #uuid "19a11638-5a2e-5378-9104-e808844efb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "weak negative linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545251} {:answer/id #uuid "89f6ee56-ed02-5b9a-9754-8229c9143dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "weak positive linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545454} {:answer/id #uuid "92d3eaca-820e-5a0b-a3c3-5153f8613c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545465} {:answer/id #uuid "e300029a-aa4d-5e7f-9e2e-d5e7ac4adb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "strong positive linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19a11638-5a2e-5378-9104-e808844efb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "weak negative linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545251} :answer-field/id #uuid "806c7b23-25c0-565c-ae67-5936dc4941f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f16d560c-09a5-5adf-953f-3ef2b521c424" :question/math-academy-id "q-133914" :question/problem "![](images/95/952ca756e237387be25ee214ba6ea2907c9af7bd6d03e9f2810648a7eb367368.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d9/d91ac29530bd2408ae92cf4ea815d1a155803b8144994184cbaca09a8c0f79e4.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *weak* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *weak negative* linear correlation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083337 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef670158-f161-51f8-b146-0c07f8a1fa5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type and Strength of a Given Correlation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c69202c-9048-5e06-90c1-b0a840de6f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "strong positive linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079992} {:answer/id #uuid "9e56b2e1-7e92-5db1-8f7b-1fe963f09434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "weak positive linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079993} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fc4e7f-d980-5ca2-9ad3-acfa44ed7e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "strong negative linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079994} {:answer/id #uuid "6236e2cb-81f0-5c22-b286-e63188769d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079995} {:answer/id #uuid "662aa487-4e22-5eb2-b233-726bea71c8c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "weak negative linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c69202c-9048-5e06-90c1-b0a840de6f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "strong positive linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079992} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc38569-cb79-51b3-81ae-ae3056e894ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dae8b0b0-26c1-5e76-af20-c4931e835d1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-133916" :question/problem "![](images/33/3397b66cdd256455a242eef55f48d59881662036f109f17d24ea9d0836097d75.png)\n\nA company produces cars. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the number of cars produced each week and the company's profit, in thousands of dollars. Which of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/6b/6b21527790fddecf89cd72a2e1d1f67338d21d82c1f3090536be973fcd60919a.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, we have a *positive* correlation.\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *strong* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *strong positive* linear correlation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083337 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef670158-f161-51f8-b146-0c07f8a1fa5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type and Strength of a Given Correlation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0ff9d9-2b5e-5df7-90da-fb81b44b5b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no linear correlation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079998} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b6ba33-66b6-5bce-9c9d